WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:04.800
Imagine you’re in the muddy trenches on&nbsp;
the western front during World War One.

00:00:04.800 --> 00:00:08.000
You’re wet and shivering and can’t get warm.

00:00:08.560 --> 00:00:14.640
Your days are marked by sleeplessness, fear,&nbsp;
and cigarettes that are too soggy to light.

00:00:15.360 --> 00:00:16.880
Death is all around you.

00:00:17.520 --> 00:00:21.760
On the other side across from no&nbsp;
man’s land are enemy soldiers.

00:00:21.760 --> 00:00:25.600
With advancements in technology&nbsp;
- your comrades are mowed down&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:25.600 --> 00:00:29.920
by killing machines in numbers beyond imagination.

00:00:29.920 --> 00:00:35.040
But you have no time to mourn…just&nbsp;
fight…lest you suffer the same fate.

00:00:35.760 --> 00:00:41.520
But then...amid unrelenting violence…the&nbsp;
bloodshed and brutality suddenly…stop.

00:00:44.240 --> 00:00:47.680
You put your weapons down – cross the enemy lines,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:47.680 --> 00:00:54.480
shake hands, and pose for photos with your&nbsp;
foe…even play a game of soccer or football!

00:00:55.120 --> 00:01:00.400
To this day, it remains one of the&nbsp;
STRANGEST moments in the history of war.

00:01:01.600 --> 00:01:07.120
On Christmas Eve, 1914, Captain Arthur&nbsp;
O’Sullivan of the British Army’s Royal&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:07.120 --> 00:01:11.360
Irish Rifles was stationed in France&nbsp;
on the northern border with Belgium.

00:01:11.360 --> 00:01:14.800
The guns were silent, and&nbsp;
he heard a German voice say:

00:01:14.800 --> 00:01:18.720
“Do not shoot after 12 o’clock&nbsp;
and we will not do so either”

00:01:19.600 --> 00:01:23.520
“If you English come out and&nbsp;
talk to us, we won’t fire.”

00:01:23.520 --> 00:01:30.000
Was it a trick? There was only one way to know&nbsp;
for sure. An Irishman dared to venture out to no&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:30.000 --> 00:01:35.280
man’s land - the area between the opposing&nbsp;
armies’ trenches - and was not shot at.

00:01:35.280 --> 00:01:39.360
When he reached the German side, an&nbsp;
enemy soldier gave him a cigarette.

00:01:39.360 --> 00:01:40.880
When he returned safely,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:40.880 --> 00:01:46.720
his comrades clambered out of the trench and went&nbsp;
to meet the Germans halfway across no man’s land.

00:01:46.720 --> 00:01:50.560
Their superiors would be forgiven&nbsp;
for reacting with more caution.

00:01:50.560 --> 00:01:55.600
Brigade headquarters cabled back warning: “It is&nbsp;
thought possible that enemy may be contemplating&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:55.600 --> 00:02:00.240
an attack during Xmas or New Year. Special&nbsp;
vigilance will be maintained during this period.”

00:02:00.960 --> 00:02:04.720
At 8:30 pm that night, Captain&nbsp;
O’Sullivan’s commanding officer,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:04.720 --> 00:02:07.840
Colonel George Laurie, reported to headquarters:

00:02:07.840 --> 00:02:11.440
“Germans have illuminated their trenches,&nbsp;
are singing songs and wishing us a Happy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:11.440 --> 00:02:17.040
Xmas. Compliments are being exchanged but am&nbsp;
nevertheless taking all military precautions.”

00:02:17.040 --> 00:02:19.680
Colonel Laurie described the Germans as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:19.680 --> 00:02:25.280
“fine men, clean and well clothed. They gave&nbsp;
us a cap and helmet badge and a box of cigars.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:26.080 --> 00:02:29.600
One of them states the war would be over in&nbsp;
three weeks as they had defeated Russia!”

00:02:30.240 --> 00:02:36.080
Despite thinking the war would be over soon,&nbsp;
World War I would actually last four years,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:36.080 --> 00:02:40.640
leaving 10 million soldiers dead&nbsp;
and about as many civilians.

00:02:41.520 --> 00:02:48.320
Yet, for a fleeting moment, good appeared to&nbsp;
triumph over evil. Instead of exchanging bullets,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:48.320 --> 00:02:51.440
sworn enemies exchanged handshakes.

00:02:51.440 --> 00:02:54.880
Colonel Laurie later recalled:&nbsp;
“You have no idea how pleasant&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:54.880 --> 00:02:57.600
everything seems with no rifle&nbsp;
bullets or shells flying about.”

00:02:58.960 --> 00:03:03.200
Further down the line, a German officer&nbsp;
who was a tenor in the Berlin Opera&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:03.200 --> 00:03:07.120
began singing “Silent Night”&nbsp;
in both German and English,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:07.120 --> 00:03:11.280
encouraging his comrades whose voices&nbsp;
flowed over to the British trenches.

00:03:11.840 --> 00:03:17.840
The contrast between the Christmas carol’s message&nbsp;
of peace and the violence of war was striking.

00:03:18.640 --> 00:03:22.880
The same spirit of camaraderie sprung&nbsp;
up in various sectors along the front&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:22.880 --> 00:03:26.240
which weaved more than 400 miles&nbsp;
through France and Belgium.

00:03:26.800 --> 00:03:32.240
Soldiers later wrote to their loved ones&nbsp;
expressing their disbelief over what had occurred.

00:03:32.240 --> 00:03:37.920
British soldier Private Frederick Heath described&nbsp;
in his letter home that he heard the Germans say:

00:03:37.920 --> 00:03:43.920
‘Come out, English soldier; come out here to us.’&nbsp;
For some little time we were cautious, and did not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:43.920 --> 00:03:50.480
even answer. Officers, fearing treachery, ordered&nbsp;
the men to be silent. But up and down our line one&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:50.480 --> 00:03:55.440
heard the men answering that Christmas greeting&nbsp;
from the enemy. How could we resist wishing each&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:55.440 --> 00:03:59.840
other a Merry Christmas, even though we might be&nbsp;
at each other’s throats immediately afterwards?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:00.720 --> 00:04:04.880
So we kept up a running conversation with&nbsp;
the Germans, all the while our hands ready&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:04.880 --> 00:04:10.880
on our rifles. Blood and peace, enmity&nbsp;
and fraternity—war’s most amazing paradox.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:12.400 --> 00:04:17.920
The night wore on to dawn—a night made easier&nbsp;
by songs from the German trenches, the pipings&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:17.920 --> 00:04:23.280
of piccolos and from our broad lines laughter&nbsp;
and Christmas carols. Not a shot was fired.

00:04:24.880 --> 00:04:27.600
The front page of the Illustrated London News&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:27.600 --> 00:04:32.800
described a German soldier approaching the British&nbsp;
lines with a small tree, as a sign of a truce.

00:04:33.760 --> 00:04:39.200
Very cautiously, Allied and German soldiers&nbsp;
climbed out of their trenches and crossed no&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:39.200 --> 00:04:44.880
man's land, where they shared drinks with their&nbsp;
enemies, as depicted in the film Joyeux Noël.

00:04:44.880 --> 00:04:48.000
I don’t think anyone would criticize us for&nbsp;
laying down our rifles on Christmas Eve.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:49.760 --> 00:04:51.680
Don’t worry, it is just for tonight.

00:04:53.680 --> 00:04:56.880
The guns remained silent on Christmas day.

00:04:56.880 --> 00:05:02.080
Although there were accounts of fighting in some&nbsp;
sectors, the Imperial War Museums believes the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:02.080 --> 00:05:07.120
truce extended along at least two-thirds of the&nbsp;
British-held trench line in southern Belgium.

00:05:07.120 --> 00:05:07.367
“The most detailed estimate, made by Malcolm&nbsp;
Brown of Britain’s Imperial War Museums,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:07.367 --> 00:05:07.556
is that the truce extended along&nbsp;
at least two-thirds of British-held&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:07.556 --> 00:05:07.680
trench line that scarred southern Belgium.”

00:05:07.680 --> 00:05:11.840
German and British soldiers can be seen&nbsp;
standing together in photographs taken&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:11.840 --> 00:05:15.520
on personal cameras that the soldiers&nbsp;
brought into the trenches with them.

00:05:15.520 --> 00:05:18.560
It was not easy for them to communicate, however,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:18.560 --> 00:05:22.560
what they DID have in common&nbsp;
was an interest in sports.

00:05:22.560 --> 00:05:27.520
A British private described “at least a couple&nbsp;
hundred” soldiers kicking around a football.

00:05:27.520 --> 00:05:31.520
This was one of several impromptu soccer&nbsp;
matches played in no man’s land. 
 &nbsp;

00:05:32.080 --> 00:05:36.800
Amongst the joy, there was sadness as&nbsp;
both sides also used this opportunity&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:36.800 --> 00:05:41.120
to recover the bodies of their fallen&nbsp;
comrades and give them a decent burial&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:41.120 --> 00:05:43.200
which they couldn’t do in the heat of battle.

00:05:43.920 --> 00:05:47.680
Headlines around the world spread&nbsp;
the news of the Christmas truce&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:47.680 --> 00:05:51.760
which must have seemed incredulous to&nbsp;
the public. Especially since British&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:51.760 --> 00:05:57.200
government propaganda portrayed German&nbsp;
soldiers as ruthless, barbaric monsters.

00:05:57.200 --> 00:05:59.840
And yet enemies were here mingling together.

00:06:00.800 --> 00:06:06.880
A German Lieutenant recalled: “How&nbsp;
marvelously wonderful, yet how strange it was.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:07.600 --> 00:06:14.000
The English officers felt the same way about&nbsp;
it. Thus Christmas, the celebration of Love,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:14.880 --> 00:06:18.960
managed to bring mortal enemies&nbsp;
together as friends for a time.”

00:06:19.760 --> 00:06:21.520
“For a time” is right.

00:06:21.520 --> 00:06:24.880
Because the next day, they went&nbsp;
back to shooting each other.

00:06:25.520 --> 00:06:30.240
Strict orders came down from the pike, and&nbsp;
they had to retreat back to their trenches.

00:06:30.240 --> 00:06:36.240
This would be the only widespread Christmas truce&nbsp;
during the course of the entire four-year war.

00:06:36.240 --> 00:06:39.920
High Commands on both sides prohibited&nbsp;
fraternizing with the enemy.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:39.920 --> 00:06:44.320
And the war became increasingly bitter&nbsp;
after the introduction of poison gas&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:44.320 --> 00:06:48.080
the following year and the number&nbsp;
of casualties began to pile up.

00:06:48.080 --> 00:06:48.800
(battles of Somme and Verdun very ugly,&nbsp;
also, poison gas was introduced in 1915)

00:06:48.800 --> 00:06:54.880
The Christmas truce of 1914 was indeed an&nbsp;
anomaly, so hard to believe that over time,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:54.880 --> 00:07:00.000
some people even doubted that it really happened,&nbsp;
despite all the evidence to the contrary.

00:07:00.000 --> 00:07:04.640
British machine gunner Bruce Bairnsfather,&nbsp;
who later became a prominent cartoonist,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:04.640 --> 00:07:07.600
depicted the truce in his work. In this cartoon,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:07.600 --> 00:07:12.640
he shows himself taking a fancy to the buttons&nbsp;
of the German officer. They agreed to do a swap.

00:07:13.200 --> 00:07:17.600
In his memoirs, Bairnsfather&nbsp;
described the incredulity of it all.

00:07:18.480 --> 00:07:22.400
“Here they were—the actual, practical&nbsp;
soldiers of the German army.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:23.600 --> 00:07:25.760
There was not an atom of hate on either side.”

00:07:26.960 --> 00:07:31.920
Even in the darkest hour, good&nbsp;
triumphed over evil, if just for a day.

00:07:32.880 --> 00:07:33.920
They had gotten to know the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:33.920 --> 00:07:34.640
enemy. And realized they were more alike&nbsp;
than the propaganda of the day would suggest.

00:07:34.640 --> 00:07:37.280
And realized they were more&nbsp;
alike than they thought.

